WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.475
Block three.

2
00:00:01.475 --> 00:00:04.175
Probability and statistics.

3
00:00:04.175 --> 00:00:08.800
On the page, this block is the section "Probability and statistics".

4
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.225
This block does two things.

5
00:00:11.225 --> 00:00:18.150
It gives you the probability language that scoring systems are written in, which is where log-odds and entropy come from.

6
00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:26.400
And it covers the statistics you need to not fool yourself: p-values, multiple testing, and what a correlation does and does not say.

7
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.225
It is the longest block, and the one that is most often needed and least often done carefully.

8
00:00:32.825 --> 00:00:35.325
Two definitions to start.

9
00:00:35.325 --> 00:00:45.525
The expectation of a random variable, written E of X, is its average over the long run: each possible value multiplied by its probability, all added up.

10
00:00:45.525 --> 00:00:49.675
Note the shape of that: value times probability, summed.

11
00:00:49.675 --> 00:00:54.800
It is a dot product, from Block one, between the values and their probabilities.

12
00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:01.525
A fair die: one through six, each with probability one-sixth, gives an expectation of three point five.

13
00:01:01.525 --> 00:01:08.100
Which is not a value the die can show, and that is fine; an expectation is an average, not a prediction.

14
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.950
The variance measures spread: the expected squared distance from the mean.

15
00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:17.025
Squared, so that being above and below the mean do not cancel.

16
00:01:17.025 --> 00:01:24.225
And the standard deviation is the square root of the variance, which brings it back into the units of the original quantity.

17
00:01:24.225 --> 00:01:27.600
For the fair die, about one point seven one.

18
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:36.700
One rule of thumb worth carrying: for a normal distribution, about ninety-five per cent of values lie within two standard deviations of the mean.

19
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.175
That is where the two in most error bars comes from.

20
00:01:40.175 --> 00:01:45.325
This is the minimum; the biostatistics course covers probability properly.

21
00:01:45.925 --> 00:01:50.750
Correlation, and two ways to measure it, which can disagree dramatically.

22
00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:54.625
Pearson's r measures how well the points fit a straight line.

23
00:01:54.625 --> 00:02:04.550
Spearman's rho is Pearson's r computed on the ranks rather than the values, so it measures whether the relationship is consistently increasing, regardless of shape.

24
00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:07.575
The left panel shows why that matters.

25
00:02:07.575 --> 00:02:17.650
Every increase in mRNA goes with an increase in protein, without exception, so Spearman's rho is exactly one: a perfect monotonic relationship.

26
00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:25.825
But one value is extreme, far from the others, and it pulls the straight-line fit around, so Pearson's r is nought point seven two.

27
00:02:25.825 --> 00:02:27.575
Same data.

28
00:02:27.575 --> 00:02:31.350
One says perfect, the other says good but not great.

29
00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:34.725
Neither is wrong; they answer different questions.

30
00:02:34.725 --> 00:02:37.600
The right panel is the opposite warning.

31
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:46.300
A U-shape: the points have a strong, obvious relationship, and both correlations are zero, because the relationship is not monotonic at all.

32
00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:50.700
Three cautions that follow, all of which you will use in Session two.

33
00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.275
Plot first.

34
00:02:52.275 --> 00:02:58.275
A correlation coefficient is a one-number summary of a picture, and you should look at the picture.

35
00:02:58.275 --> 00:03:02.750
A correlation has a p-value, and it depends on the number of points.

36
00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:06.250
With five points, almost nothing is significant.

37
00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:12.850
And correlation is not causation, which you know, and which remains the most common error in the literature.

38
00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:20.675
Conditional probability, and the direction of it, which is where most statistical confusion in biology comes from.

39
00:03:20.675 --> 00:03:26.225
P of A given B means: among the cases where B is true, what fraction also have A?

40
00:03:26.225 --> 00:03:30.450
You restrict your attention to B, and ask about A within that.

41
00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:35.200
The formula is the probability of both, divided by the probability of B.

42
00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.350
Here is the worked example from the slide.

43
00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:40.675
Two hundred proteins.

44
00:03:40.675 --> 00:03:46.100
Sixty are membrane proteins, and thirty of those sixty have a signal peptide.

45
00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.575
Of the other hundred and forty, twenty have one.

46
00:03:49.575 --> 00:03:57.925
So: the probability of having a signal peptide given that it is a membrane protein is thirty out of sixty, which is nought point five.

47
00:03:57.925 --> 00:04:04.525
And the other direction: the probability of being a membrane protein given that it has a signal peptide.

48
00:04:04.525 --> 00:04:12.150
There are fifty proteins with a signal peptide in total, thirty plus twenty, and thirty of those are membrane proteins.

49
00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:15.550
Thirty out of fifty, which is nought point six.

50
00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:18.575
Nought point five and nought point six.

51
00:04:18.575 --> 00:04:22.125
Same data, different questions, different answers.

52
00:04:22.125 --> 00:04:25.150
The direction is not a technicality.

53
00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:32.325
And this is exactly the mistake behind the most common misreading of p-values, which we come to at the end of this block.

54
00:04:32.325 --> 00:04:40.800
A p-value is the probability of the data given the hypothesis, and people read it as the probability of the hypothesis given the data.

55
00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.850
Those are the two directions of this slide.

56
00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:48.175
A profile gives you the probability of a residue given a position.

57
00:04:48.175 --> 00:04:52.500
A classifier gives you the probability of a class given an input.

58
00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:57.850
Bayes' rule converts one into the other, and it is in the optional box on the page.

59
00:04:58.450 --> 00:05:04.825
Why this field computes with logarithms, which has one very practical reason and one elegant one.

60
00:05:04.825 --> 00:05:10.100
The practical reason: probabilities of sequences are unimaginably small.

61
00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:16.750
Take a particular thousand-base sequence under a uniform model, where each base has probability a quarter.

62
00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:25.225
The probability of that exact sequence is nought point two five to the power of a thousand, which is about ten to the minus six hundred and two.

63
00:05:25.225 --> 00:05:30.300
A computer storing an ordinary floating-point number cannot represent that.

64
00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.425
It rounds to zero.

65
00:05:32.425 --> 00:05:39.225
And then every subsequent calculation gives zero, and your program runs perfectly and produces nothing.

66
00:05:39.225 --> 00:05:45.225
Take the logarithm, and ten to the minus six hundred and two becomes minus six hundred and two.

67
00:05:45.225 --> 00:05:47.900
An entirely ordinary number.

68
00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:52.900
The elegant reason: the logarithm of a product is the sum of the logarithms.

69
00:05:52.900 --> 00:06:00.325
So multiplying probabilities, which is what you do for independent positions, becomes adding log-probabilities.

70
00:06:00.325 --> 00:06:05.925
Additions are faster than multiplications, and more importantly they do not underflow.

71
00:06:05.925 --> 00:06:11.200
That is why scores in this field are added along a sequence rather than multiplied.

72
00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:20.775
Every alignment score, every profile score, every log-odds score you meet is a sum of logarithms, because the underlying model multiplies probabilities.

73
00:06:20.775 --> 00:06:31.025
One footnote: logs to different bases differ only by a constant factor, so base two, base ten and natural logs are interchangeable as long as you are consistent.

74
00:06:31.025 --> 00:06:33.350
Base two gives you bits.

75
00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:38.625
And here is the scoring system that the first half of the course is built on.

76
00:06:38.625 --> 00:06:47.275
A log-odds score compares two models: how likely is this observation under the thing I am looking for, against how likely under the background?

77
00:06:47.275 --> 00:06:52.150
Take the ratio, take the logarithm, and add those up over the positions.

78
00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:54.025
Worked example.

79
00:06:54.025 --> 00:07:05.200
A GC-rich model where G and C each have probability nought point three five and A and T each nought point one five, against a uniform background of nought point two five each.

80
00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:12.175
Per base: log to base two of nought point three five over nought point two five is plus nought point four nine.

81
00:07:12.175 --> 00:07:19.175
And log to base two of nought point one five over nought point two five is minus nought point seven four.

82
00:07:19.175 --> 00:07:25.975
So a G or a C scores plus nought point four nine, and an A or a T scores minus nought point seven four.

83
00:07:25.975 --> 00:07:29.150
Now score the sequence G C G C A T.

84
00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:35.325
Four bases scoring plus nought point four nine, and two scoring minus nought point seven four.

85
00:07:35.325 --> 00:07:43.350
Four times nought point four nine is one point nine six; two times minus nought point seven four is minus one point four eight.

86
00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:46.575
Total plus nought point four seven bits.

87
00:07:46.575 --> 00:07:58.975
And you can interpret that directly: two to the power of nought point four seven is about one point four, so this sequence is about one point four times more likely under the GC-rich model than under the background.

88
00:07:58.975 --> 00:08:01.700
Positive means the model wins.

89
00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:05.150
That is what every substitution matrix is.

90
00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:14.100
BLOSUM scores are log-odds: how often do these two residues align in real homologous proteins, against how often by chance?

91
00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:16.325
Sessions four and five.

92
00:08:16.325 --> 00:08:25.475
And volcano plots use minus log ten of a p-value for the same reason: it turns a range spanning many orders of magnitude into a readable axis.

93
00:08:26.075 --> 00:08:29.200
A short slide tying two things together.

94
00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:34.500
We have just seen that log-odds are a natural way to combine evidence: add them up.

95
00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:38.825
But at the end you often want a probability, not a log-odds score.

96
00:08:38.825 --> 00:08:42.100
The function that converts back is the sigmoid.

97
00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:51.700
Precisely: take a probability p, form the odds, p over one minus p, take the natural logarithm to get the log-odds, and then apply the sigmoid to that.

98
00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.550
You get p back.

99
00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:57.875
The sigmoid is exactly the inverse of the log-odds transformation.

100
00:08:57.875 --> 00:09:01.575
Which explains something that otherwise looks arbitrary.

101
00:09:01.575 --> 00:09:07.550
In Session eight, logistic regression computes a weighted sum and then applies a sigmoid.

102
00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:11.100
Why a sigmoid and not some other squashing function?

103
00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:20.950
Because the weighted sum is being interpreted as a log-odds score, built by adding up evidence, and the sigmoid is what turns a log-odds score into a probability.

104
00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:29.900
One caution we repeat in Session eight: the output is a calibrated probability only if the model was fitted for that, with the appropriate loss.

105
00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:36.575
Applying a sigmoid to an arbitrary score gives a number between nought and one that is not a probability.

106
00:09:37.175 --> 00:09:42.800
Likelihood, which is the idea behind how essentially every model in this course is fitted.

107
00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:45.275
Turn probability around.

108
00:09:45.275 --> 00:09:49.500
Normally you have a model and ask how probable some data is.

109
00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:57.050
Likelihood fixes the data, which you have observed, and asks: for which parameter values would this data have been probable?

110
00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:58.925
Worked example.

111
00:09:58.925 --> 00:10:02.175
Ten bases, of which seven are G or C.

112
00:10:02.175 --> 00:10:07.950
Assume the positions are independent, with some unknown probability p of being G or C.

113
00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:13.075
The likelihood of the data is p to the seventh, times one minus p, cubed.

114
00:10:13.075 --> 00:10:15.200
Now try values.

115
00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:20.775
For p equal to nought point five: about nought point zero zero zero nine eight.

116
00:10:20.775 --> 00:10:26.100
For p equal to nought point seven: about nought point zero zero two two.

117
00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:31.750
So nought point seven is about two point three times more likely than nought point five.

118
00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:39.375
And no other value of p does better than nought point seven, which is exactly the observed fraction, seven out of ten.

119
00:10:39.375 --> 00:10:45.350
That is maximum likelihood: choose the parameters that make the observed data most probable.

120
00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:53.975
It is how profiles are built, how substitution matrices are derived, and, as the next slide shows, what training a classifier actually is.

121
00:10:53.975 --> 00:11:03.250
In practice you always compute with logs, for the reasons two slides back, which is why so many loss functions in this course are negative log-likelihoods.

122
00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:09.925
Entropy, which is the measure behind sequence logos and behind every classifier loss.

123
00:11:09.925 --> 00:11:12.000
Start with surprise.

124
00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:19.575
If something happens that had probability p, the surprise of it is minus log to base two of p, measured in bits.

125
00:11:19.575 --> 00:11:22.125
Check that it behaves sensibly.

126
00:11:22.125 --> 00:11:29.075
Something certain, probability one, has surprise minus log of one, which is zero bits: no information.

127
00:11:29.075 --> 00:11:32.725
A fair coin, probability a half, gives one bit.

128
00:11:32.725 --> 00:11:36.450
One of four equally likely bases gives two bits.

129
00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:43.425
Entropy, written H, is the average surprise: each outcome's surprise weighted by its probability, summed.

130
00:11:43.425 --> 00:11:48.325
So entropy is high when outcomes are unpredictable and low when they are not.

131
00:11:48.325 --> 00:11:52.200
Four equally likely bases: two bits, the maximum.

132
00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:55.750
A position that is always G: zero bits.

133
00:11:55.750 --> 00:11:58.650
And here is where you have already seen it.

134
00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:08.950
In a sequence logo, the height of the stack at each position is the maximum entropy minus the actual entropy: how much less uncertain this position is than a random one.

135
00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:12.800
A tall stack means a conserved, predictable position.

136
00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:14.925
That is Session four.

137
00:12:14.925 --> 00:12:16.650
Two footnotes.

138
00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:25.150
Session twelve's direct coupling analysis fits a maximum-entropy model, which means the least committed model consistent with what you measured.

139
00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:32.725
And the entropy of protein folding in Session six has the same mathematical form but is a different physical quantity.

140
00:12:33.325 --> 00:12:37.250
And now the one that matters most for the second half of the course.

141
00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:45.400
Cross-entropy measures the average surprise when the data really comes from one distribution, P, but your model believes another, Q.

142
00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:50.975
You weight by the true probabilities and take the surprise under your model's probabilities.

143
00:12:50.975 --> 00:12:52.775
Two properties.

144
00:12:52.775 --> 00:12:56.225
Cross-entropy is never smaller than the true entropy of P.

145
00:12:56.225 --> 00:13:00.375
And it is equal to it only when your model is exactly right.

146
00:13:00.375 --> 00:13:04.975
So minimising cross-entropy means making your model match reality.

147
00:13:04.975 --> 00:13:09.200
The worked example: data that is seventy per cent G or C.

148
00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:14.850
If your model says fifty per cent, the cross-entropy is one point zero zero bit.

149
00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:20.725
If your model says seventy per cent, it is nought point eight eight bits, which is the minimum.

150
00:13:20.725 --> 00:13:23.250
Any other value is worse.

151
00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:27.875
And now compare with the likelihood slide, because these are the same thing.

152
00:13:27.875 --> 00:13:31.300
There, nought point seven gave the highest likelihood.

153
00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:34.900
Here, nought point seven gives the lowest cross-entropy.

154
00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:48.650
In fact log to base two of the likelihood at nought point five was minus ten bits for ten bases, which is minus one bit each, and at nought point seven about minus eight point eight bits, which is minus nought point eight eight each.

155
00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:53.050
Cross-entropy is the negative log-likelihood per observation.

156
00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:55.800
Same quantity, opposite sign.

157
00:13:55.800 --> 00:14:05.875
So when Session eight tells you to train a classifier with cross-entropy loss, it is telling you to do maximum likelihood, which is the thing statisticians have done for a century.

158
00:14:05.875 --> 00:14:17.500
And the gap between the cross-entropy and the true entropy, the extra bits you pay for having the wrong model, is the relative entropy, also called the KL divergence, which you met in Session four.

159
00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:24.050
Now the statistics for not fooling yourself, starting with the most misused number in science.

160
00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:30.775
A p-value is the probability of observing data at least this extreme, if the null hypothesis were true.

161
00:14:30.775 --> 00:14:34.000
It is an area in the tail of a distribution.

162
00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:41.675
Say that definition again, slowly, and notice its direction: it is the probability of the data, given the hypothesis.

163
00:14:41.675 --> 00:14:45.875
It is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true.

164
00:14:45.875 --> 00:14:55.525
That is the other direction of the conditional probability slide, and converting between them requires Bayes' rule and a prior, which a p-value does not contain.

165
00:14:55.525 --> 00:15:01.025
A small p-value says: if nothing were going on, data like this would be unusual.

166
00:15:01.025 --> 00:15:04.975
It does not say how likely it is that nothing is going on.

167
00:15:04.975 --> 00:15:09.150
And nought point zero five is a convention, nothing more.

168
00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:18.850
It was a suggestion, it became a ritual, and there is no mathematics in which nought point zero four nine is meaningfully different from nought point zero five one.

169
00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:29.600
What the threshold does mean, precisely: if the null hypothesis is true and all the test's assumptions hold, using nought point zero five will reject it five per cent of the time.

170
00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:35.875
That is a statement about the long-run behaviour of the procedure, not about your particular experiment.

171
00:15:35.875 --> 00:15:47.475
The worked example on the slide: Welch's t-test on four controls and four treated samples gives t about minus eleven point nine and p about nought point zero zero zero one.

172
00:15:47.475 --> 00:15:53.225
We use the same numbers again in Session two, with the SciPy function that computes them.

173
00:15:53.825 --> 00:15:59.700
And the thing that makes p-values dangerous in our field specifically: we do not run one test.

174
00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:01.700
We run thousands.

175
00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:04.750
Here is the arithmetic that should worry you.

176
00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:13.075
If the null hypothesis is true, p-values are uniformly distributed: every value between nought and one is equally likely.

177
00:16:13.075 --> 00:16:18.200
So five per cent of them fall below nought point zero five, by construction.

178
00:16:18.200 --> 00:16:25.675
Test two thousand genes where nothing is happening, and about a hundred of them will have p below nought point zero five.

179
00:16:25.675 --> 00:16:29.100
A hundred significant results from pure noise.

180
00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:31.550
Two standard responses.

181
00:16:31.550 --> 00:16:35.475
Bonferroni: divide your threshold by the number of tests.

182
00:16:35.475 --> 00:16:40.875
For two thousand tests, that is nought point zero zero zero zero two five.

183
00:16:40.875 --> 00:16:50.325
It is correct and it is usually far too strict: it controls the probability of even one false positive, which is rarely what you want when you are screening.

184
00:16:50.325 --> 00:16:54.575
The false discovery rate is better suited to our situation.

185
00:16:54.575 --> 00:17:02.375
Instead of preventing any false positive, control the expected proportion of false positives among the things you call significant.

186
00:17:02.375 --> 00:17:11.475
Benjamini-Hochberg is the standard procedure, and it controls that under stated assumptions, for independent or positively correlated tests.

187
00:17:11.475 --> 00:17:21.825
The shift in thinking is worth stating: you stop asking is this one result real and start asking of this list I am about to publish, what fraction is likely to be wrong.

188
00:17:22.425 --> 00:17:27.900
The standard picture for this, which you will see in every differential expression paper.

189
00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:30.000
A volcano plot.

190
00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.725
On the horizontal axis, the log two fold change: how much the gene changed, and in which direction.

191
00:17:36.725 --> 00:17:44.400
On the vertical axis, minus log ten of the p-value: how much evidence there is, with the most significant genes highest.

192
00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:47.300
Two axes, two different questions.

193
00:17:47.300 --> 00:17:50.500
The horizontal is the size of the effect.

194
00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:53.700
The vertical is the strength of the evidence.

195
00:17:53.700 --> 00:18:03.025
A gene can be strongly changed with weak evidence, if it is noisy, or barely changed with overwhelming evidence, if it is measured very precisely.

196
00:18:03.025 --> 00:18:08.300
The interesting genes are the ones that are both: far out to one side and high up.

197
00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:13.500
And the reason this plot exists is that reporting only one axis misleads.

198
00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:19.975
A list ranked by p-value alone will be full of genes that changed by two per cent, very reliably.

199
00:18:19.975 --> 00:18:28.600
The data here is a simulation: two thousand genes, of which fifty are genuinely differentially expressed, with a Welch t-test for each.

200
00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:33.025
Which means we know the right answer, and the next slide uses that.

201
00:18:33.625 --> 00:18:38.425
And here is why the previous slide's correction matters, with the truth known.

202
00:18:38.425 --> 00:18:42.650
Take the naive threshold, p below nought point zero five.

203
00:18:42.650 --> 00:18:45.600
You get a hundred and thirty-five hits.

204
00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:48.350
Of those, eighty-five are false.

205
00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:51.950
Eighty-five false out of a hundred and thirty-five.

206
00:18:51.950 --> 00:18:54.825
Well over half your list is noise.

207
00:18:54.825 --> 00:19:01.325
And if you did not know the truth, as you do not in a real experiment, you would have no way of telling which half.

208
00:19:01.325 --> 00:19:03.900
Where do eighty-five come from?

209
00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:12.250
There are nineteen hundred and fifty genes where nothing is happening, and five per cent of nineteen hundred and fifty is about ninety-seven.

210
00:19:12.250 --> 00:19:14.850
Eighty-five is close to that.

211
00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:19.950
Exactly as predicted; nothing has gone wrong except our expectations.

212
00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:26.600
Now Benjamini-Hochberg at five per cent: forty-four hits, and in this simulation all of them are real.

213
00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:29.750
Fewer discoveries, and a list you can act on.

214
00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:34.650
One honest caution about that result, which I want to be precise about.

215
00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:43.900
Controlling the false discovery rate at five per cent does not promise zero false positives; it promises about five per cent of them on average.

216
00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:50.100
That none of these forty-four is false is a property of this particular simulation, not a guarantee.

217
00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:52.275
Do not quote it as one.

218
00:19:52.875 --> 00:19:56.700
Before the summary, something current, and a question for you.

219
00:19:56.700 --> 00:20:09.100
In twenty twenty-four, AlphaProof and AlphaGeometry 2 solved four of the six problems at the International Mathematical Olympiad, scoring twenty-eight out of forty-two, one point below a gold medal.

220
00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:25.700
In twenty twenty-five, an advanced version of Gemini Deep Think solved five of six, in natural language, within the official time limit: thirty-five out of forty-two, gold-medal standard, and graded by the olympiad's own coordinators, which is the part that makes it checkable.

221
00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:31.950
OpenAI reported the same score for an experimental model, graded by itself, which is not.

222
00:20:31.950 --> 00:20:44.400
And in twenty twenty-six, OpenAI released three hundred and seventy-two results described as solving an open problem or making substantial progress towards one, at roughly three hours of compute each.

223
00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:50.050
I want you to hear that last one as a claim rather than a result, and to understand why.

224
00:20:50.050 --> 00:20:55.950
Many come with formal proofs in Lean, which is a proof assistant that machine-checks the logic.

225
00:20:55.950 --> 00:20:59.825
That is real: it means the argument contains no gap.

226
00:20:59.825 --> 00:21:05.775
But checking the logic is not the same as establishing that a result is new, or that it matters.

227
00:21:05.775 --> 00:21:12.100
Both of those require a mathematician who knows the field, and mathematicians are still reading them.

228
00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:15.725
Reports differ on how many are complete solutions.

229
00:21:15.725 --> 00:21:21.650
Which brings the question on the slide: if an AI can do the mathematics, why should you learn it?

230
00:21:21.650 --> 00:21:25.675
One answer, and it is the same answer the whole course gives.

231
00:21:25.675 --> 00:21:31.200
To judge whether a result is right, and whether it answers the question you actually asked.

232
00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:39.025
That is precisely what Sessions eight and nine teach you to do for models, and it is not a skill you can delegate to the thing being judged.

233
00:21:39.625 --> 00:21:42.200
The summary, as three columns.

234
00:21:42.200 --> 00:21:44.175
Linear algebra.

235
00:21:44.175 --> 00:21:49.775
The dot product is a weighted sum, and it is both a profile score and a perceptron.

236
00:21:49.775 --> 00:21:53.325
Matrix products score a whole dataset at once.

237
00:21:53.325 --> 00:21:58.125
And the advice that will save you the most time: know the shape of everything.

238
00:21:58.125 --> 00:21:59.675
Calculus.

239
00:21:59.675 --> 00:22:06.275
The gradient points uphill, so you step against it, and that is how every model in this course is trained.

240
00:22:06.275 --> 00:22:11.700
The chain rule multiplies derivatives along a chain, and that is backpropagation.

241
00:22:11.700 --> 00:22:13.425
Probability.

242
00:22:13.425 --> 00:22:17.875
Logs turn products into sums, which is why scores are added.

243
00:22:17.875 --> 00:22:24.500
Log-odds compare a model against a background, which is what substitution matrices and profiles are.

244
00:22:24.500 --> 00:22:31.400
Cross-entropy is maximum likelihood with a sign change, and it is the loss for every classifier you will build.

245
00:22:31.400 --> 00:22:41.550
And the p-value cautions: it is the probability of the data given the hypothesis, not the other way round, and when you run thousands of tests you must correct for it.

246
00:22:41.550 --> 00:22:47.400
If you can give one example of where each of these comes back, you are ready for the rest of the course.

247
00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:50.075
That is Session one.

248
00:22:50.075 --> 00:22:57.075
Session two moves from mathematics to practice: how a computational biologist actually works in Python.

249
00:22:57.075 --> 00:23:08.375
Choosing the right object for your data, exploring a real dataset before computing on it, using libraries rather than writing your own, and having a routine for when code does not work.

250
00:23:08.375 --> 00:23:15.275
There is also live coding, in which I make three mistakes on purpose, and only one of them produces an error.

251
00:23:15.275 --> 00:23:22.175
Its message, which is worth hearing twice: code that runs without an error does not mean the analysis is correct.

252
00:23:22.175 --> 00:23:35.875
The Session one page has all of this written out, with worked solutions to every exercise under its "Solutions" headings, and the course notebook, which computes every number I have quoted, is linked in the box under the page's title.

253
00:23:35.875 --> 00:23:37.500
See you there.

